Below is a **compact, self‑contained** C# program that demonstrates the most common ways to use pattern matching in modern C# (C# 9‑11).  
It shows:

| Feature | Syntax | What it does |
|---------|--------|--------------|
| **Type pattern** | `case Person p:` | Binds `p` if the object is a `Person`. |
| **Relational pattern** | `case int i when i > 10:` | Tests a value against a relational constraint. |
| **Property pattern** | `case Person { Age: >= 18 }:` | Matches on a property of a type. |
| **Positional pattern** | `case (int x, int y):` | Matches a `ValueTuple` (or any deconstructable type). |
| **Logical pattern** | `case null or EmptyString:` | Combines patterns with `or`, `and`, `not`. |
| **Switch expression** | `var result = x switch { ... };` | Concise return‑style switch. |
| **`is` with pattern** | `if (obj is string s)` | Quick check + local variable. |

```csharp
using System;

// A small type hierarchy to pattern‑match against
abstract record Shape;

record Circle(double Radius) : Shape;
record Rectangle(double Width, double Height) : Shape;
record Triangle(double A, double B, double C) : Shape;

// Simple helper
static string EmptyString => string.Empty;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // 1. Type, relational and property patterns inside a switch statement
        Shape shape = new Circle(5.0);
        Console.WriteLine($"Area of the shape: {shape switch
        {
            Circle c when c.Radius > 0 => Math.PI * c.Radius * c.Radius,
            Rectangle r => r.Width * r.Height,
            Triangle t when IsValidTriangle(t) => 0.5 * t.A * t.B,   // silly area formula
            _ => 0
        }}");

        // 2. Property pattern (matching on a nested property)
        var person = new Person { Name = "Alice", Age = 28 };
        if (person is { Age: >= 18, Name: var name })
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"{name} is an adult.");
        }

        // 3. Positional pattern (matching a ValueTuple)
        var point = (x: 3, y: 4);
        var (x, y) = point; // normal deconstruction
        if (point is (int px, int py) && px == 3 && py == 4)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Point is at ({px},{py})");
        }

        // 4. Logical pattern
        string? maybeNull = null;
        if (maybeNull is null or EmptyString)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("The string is null or empty.");
        }

        // 5. `is` with pattern
        object obj = "Hello, World!";
        if (obj is string s)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"String length: {s.Length}");
        }

        // 6. Switch expression returning a value
        int score = 85;
        string grade = score switch
        {
            >= 90 => "A",
            >= 80 => "B",
            >= 70 => "C",
            >= 60 => "D",
            _     => "F"
        };
        Console.WriteLine($"Score {score} => Grade {grade}");
    }

    // A tiny helper to check if a triangle is valid
    static bool IsValidTriangle(Triangle t) =>
        t.A + t.B > t.C && t.A + t.C > t.B && t.B + t.C > t.A;
}

record Person(string Name, int Age);
```

### Quick highlights

1. **Switch expression** (`shape switch { … }`) is the most concise way to get a value from pattern matching.
2. **Property pattern** (`{ Age: >= 18, Name: var name }`) lets you match *inside* an object without writing an explicit `is` block.
3. **Positional pattern** works on any type that supports deconstruction (`(int x, int y)`).
4. **Logical pattern** (`null or EmptyString`) lets you combine patterns with `or`, `and`, `not`.
5. **`is` with pattern** (`if (obj is string s)`) provides a one‑liner cast‑and‑check that introduces a local variable.

Feel free to copy the code into a .NET 7+ project (or the online C# sandbox) and experiment with different shapes, ages, and score values to see how the pattern matching logic adapts!
